1
00:00:02,291 --> 00:00:05,255
So what is TypeScript and why

2
00:00:05,800 --> 00:00:06,425
would we use it?

3
00:00:07,242 --> 00:00:10,992
TypeScript is a superset to JavaScript. That

4
00:00:10,992 --> 00:00:14,116
simply means that it extends JavaScript. It

5
00:00:14,132 --> 00:00:17,738
builds up on JavaScript. And unlike

6
00:00:18,042 --> 00:00:21,343
JavaScript, TypeScript does not run in the

7
00:00:21,407 --> 00:00:24,548
browser. Instead, TypeScript has to be

8
00:00:24,548 --> 00:00:28,091
compiled to JavaScript that it runs again.

9
00:00:28,797 --> 00:00:31,654
Now, why would we then work with it? Because

10
00:00:31,766 --> 00:00:35,056
TypeScript gives us as a developer a better

11
00:00:35,056 --> 00:00:37,592
development experience because it adds

12
00:00:37,721 --> 00:00:40,722
certain features to the code which only

13
00:00:40,738 --> 00:00:43,354
exist during development but which still

14
00:00:43,563 --> 00:00:47,382
help us write better code and avoid unwanted

15
00:00:47,639 --> 00:00:47,944
errors.

16
00:00:48,859 --> 00:00:51,716
And here's an example. This is a function,

17
00:00:51,828 --> 00:00:54,572
this is some code where TypeScript can help

18
00:00:54,700 --> 00:00:54,764
you.

19
00:00:55,759 --> 00:00:57,267
Now, what could be wrong about this?

20
00:00:58,085 --> 00:00:59,577
Let's simply try it on our own.

21
00:01:00,556 --> 00:01:02,369
For this, you can simply open up your

22
00:01:02,417 --> 00:01:05,048
developer tools in Chrome, for example, and

23
00:01:05,048 --> 00:01:06,957
there go to the JavaScript console.

24
00:01:07,535 --> 00:01:09,556
In there, you can also write some basic

25
00:01:09,556 --> 00:01:12,123
JavaScript code which will then be executed

26
00:01:12,123 --> 00:01:13,904
here in the browser. And for this simple

27
00:01:13,904 --> 00:01:15,219
example, that's all we need.

28
00:01:15,909 --> 00:01:18,637
So in there, let's define a function now

29
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:20,995
A function which is named add

30
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:25,489
and which takes 2 parameters, num1 and num2.

31
00:01:26,468 --> 00:01:28,843
Then let's add curly braces and now place

32
00:01:28,955 --> 00:01:31,539
your cursor between these braces and hit

33
00:01:31,539 --> 00:01:32,742
Shift+Enter.

34
00:01:33,609 --> 00:01:35,310
This will then simply add a new line.

35
00:01:36,514 --> 00:01:38,552
Now inside of that function, we can return

36
00:01:38,600 --> 00:01:42,114
the result, num1 plus num2, just like this.

37
00:01:42,548 --> 00:01:44,987
So that's a very trivial function for adding

38
00:01:45,099 --> 00:01:45,709
2 numbers.

39
00:01:46,462 --> 00:01:48,529
If you now hit Enter without Shift,

40
00:01:49,170 --> 00:01:49,923
this code will be

41
00:01:50,452 --> 00:01:52,615
committed, it will be saved, and now this

42
00:01:52,695 --> 00:01:54,089
add function exists here

43
00:01:54,618 --> 00:01:55,500
in the console.

44
00:01:56,076 --> 00:01:58,945
Hence, we can now call add with 1 and 5 and

45
00:01:59,025 --> 00:02:01,188
get back 6. Seems to work.

46
00:02:02,229 --> 00:02:05,947
What if you would pass in 1 and 5 as strings

47
00:02:06,091 --> 00:02:06,171
though?

48
00:02:07,052 --> 00:02:11,427
Then you get back the string 15. So 1 and 5

49
00:02:11,603 --> 00:02:14,792
are then concatenated to one longer string

50
00:02:15,546 --> 00:02:18,255
instead of being converted to numbers and

51
00:02:18,431 --> 00:02:19,762
then added as numbers.

52
00:02:20,676 --> 00:02:23,016
Now the reason for this is that this is

53
00:02:23,016 --> 00:02:25,774
simply how JavaScript works. If you have

54
00:02:26,351 --> 00:02:29,108
an operation with a plus and at least one of

55
00:02:29,108 --> 00:02:30,663
the 2 operands is a string,

56
00:02:31,240 --> 00:02:34,350
then both will be combined as strings

57
00:02:34,383 --> 00:02:36,531
essentially. That's how JavaScript works.

58
00:02:37,108 --> 00:02:38,791
And this of course can be a problem here.

59
00:02:39,417 --> 00:02:41,648
Now you might think that this is an

60
00:02:41,680 --> 00:02:44,296
unrealistic scenario because why would you

61
00:02:44,312 --> 00:02:45,901
call the function with 2 strings?

62
00:02:46,575 --> 00:02:49,560
Well, imagine that you have 2 inputs in your

63
00:02:49,624 --> 00:02:49,993
page,

64
00:02:50,571 --> 00:02:53,042
2 inputs where you fetch some user input.

65
00:02:53,733 --> 00:02:56,750
Now you should know that whatever data you

66
00:02:56,766 --> 00:03:00,425
extract from such inputs is always extracted

67
00:03:00,521 --> 00:03:03,972
as text in JavaScript. So even if the user

68
00:03:04,132 --> 00:03:07,004
entered a number in an input on your page,

69
00:03:07,357 --> 00:03:09,812
if you extract that data with JavaScript,

70
00:03:10,309 --> 00:03:11,335
it'll be a string.

71
00:03:12,426 --> 00:03:14,079
Now you can always convert this to a number,

72
00:03:14,095 --> 00:03:16,340
of course, but if you forget this and you

73
00:03:16,340 --> 00:03:19,132
pass the unconverted value to this function,

74
00:03:19,549 --> 00:03:22,003
you might get this unintended result.

75
00:03:23,383 --> 00:03:25,773
Now that's where TypeScript can help us to

76
00:03:25,949 --> 00:03:28,821
avoid such an unwanted behavior which occurs

77
00:03:29,077 --> 00:03:33,073
at runtime. So when our code executes we can

78
00:03:33,249 --> 00:03:35,722
use TypeScript. Now, of course, we could

79
00:03:35,899 --> 00:03:38,580
also avoid this behavior at runtime with

80
00:03:38,628 --> 00:03:39,287
JavaScript.

81
00:03:39,832 --> 00:03:41,936
We could add an if check, for example, to

82
00:03:41,936 --> 00:03:44,376
see whether the values we received are

83
00:03:44,441 --> 00:03:47,010
strings or numbers. That is something we can

84
00:03:47,074 --> 00:03:49,723
check with JavaScript, and this would allow

85
00:03:49,723 --> 00:03:52,276
us to avoid such mistakes. But of course,

86
00:03:52,292 --> 00:03:54,701
that means that you have to write extra code

87
00:03:54,701 --> 00:03:56,997
at runtime to make sure that your code

88
00:03:57,093 --> 00:03:57,398
works.

89
00:03:58,217 --> 00:04:00,430
When you actually could avoid this during

90
00:04:00,478 --> 00:04:03,573
development if you had strict type checks.

91
00:04:04,022 --> 00:04:05,481
So if you simply could tell

92
00:04:06,331 --> 00:04:09,105
JavaScript, so to say, in advance which

93
00:04:09,217 --> 00:04:12,905
types of data you want, and then JavaScript

94
00:04:12,986 --> 00:04:15,808
and your IDE could warn you when you have

95
00:04:15,808 --> 00:04:18,951
some code in your program where wrong types

96
00:04:19,143 --> 00:04:19,737
are fed in.

97
00:04:20,522 --> 00:04:22,655
And that's where TypeScript helps us. It

98
00:04:22,912 --> 00:04:24,419
adds strict typing.

99
00:04:25,092 --> 00:04:27,738
In general, TypeScript adds a bunch of

100
00:04:27,738 --> 00:04:29,342
features to JavaScript.

101
00:04:29,887 --> 00:04:31,763
It adds the types and that's the most

102
00:04:31,763 --> 00:04:33,993
important addition. That's where

103
00:04:33,993 --> 00:04:35,869
TypeScript's name comes from.

104
00:04:36,783 --> 00:04:39,814
But it also unlocks certain next-generation

105
00:04:39,846 --> 00:04:42,299
JavaScript features which we then can use in

106
00:04:42,411 --> 00:04:45,683
our code without any extra tools like Babel.

107
00:04:46,821 --> 00:04:50,590
It also adds some non-next-gen features, so

108
00:04:50,590 --> 00:04:52,562
some features which don't exist in

109
00:04:52,594 --> 00:04:54,807
JavaScript at all, which help us write

110
00:04:54,839 --> 00:04:55,609
cleaner code.

111
00:04:56,300 --> 00:04:58,580
Now, all these features are stripped away

112
00:04:58,596 --> 00:05:00,604
once it's compiled, but during development,

113
00:05:00,684 --> 00:05:03,174
those features can help us write cleaner

114
00:05:03,174 --> 00:05:03,479
code.

115
00:05:04,475 --> 00:05:06,562
It adds metaprogramming features like

116
00:05:06,562 --> 00:05:07,253
decorators.

117
00:05:07,912 --> 00:05:10,851
It has rich configuration options that allow

118
00:05:11,124 --> 00:05:14,015
us as a developer to fine-tune how code

119
00:05:14,015 --> 00:05:17,002
should be compiled. And it in general simply

120
00:05:17,066 --> 00:05:19,154
gives us a lot of modern tooling and

121
00:05:19,251 --> 00:05:22,431
integrates greatly into modern tooling to

122
00:05:22,463 --> 00:05:24,599
give us a great development experience.

123
00:05:25,354 --> 00:05:28,277
Now in this basics refresher here,

124
00:05:28,807 --> 00:05:31,424
I'm not going to dive into all these areas

125
00:05:31,457 --> 00:05:32,356
in great detail.

126
00:05:33,304 --> 00:05:35,359
For that, you can check out my Understanding

127
00:05:35,391 --> 00:05:36,339
TypeScript course.

128
00:05:36,997 --> 00:05:39,487
This course dives into TypeScript in great

129
00:05:39,487 --> 00:05:42,554
detail and walks you through all the core

130
00:05:42,779 --> 00:05:45,028
features. So that's my recommendation if you

131
00:05:45,028 --> 00:05:46,794
wanna learn all about TypeScript.

132
00:05:47,677 --> 00:05:49,701
Here we're going to dive into some core

